+// GPU renderer (Ape Escape 2 recomp patch 0047): the GS drawn with OpenGL 3.3 core.
+//
+// A port of the PAL port's GPU renderer (JackyPendrag0n/ae2-recomp, port/src/gs_hw.h and gl.h, used with the
+// author's permission) to this runtime's raster backend interface. It is the default; APE2_GS_RENDERER=cpu selects the software
+// renderer. It needs the GS thread (APE2_MTGS=2, the default) and falls back to software without a GL context.
+//
+//   - The software backend (GSCpuBackend) stays the owner of VRAM: transfers, CLUT loads and texture decoding
+//     run there as before. Only primitives are drawn on the GPU.
+//   - Render targets live in GPU textures keyed by frame-buffer address/width/format; depth buffers likewise
+//     (32-bit float depth = Z / 2^32). Primitives with the same state are drawn in one call.
+//   - VRAM stays the authority for everything the CPU reads: before a texture decode, CLUT load or transfer
+//     reads VRAM that a target has drawn over, that target is read back; a texture whose address is a render
+//     target samples the target directly (render-to-texture effects); uploads into a target are pushed to it.
+//   - The displayed frame buffer is read back into VRAM when the game swaps buffers and at each vsync mark,
+//     and the existing presentation path shows VRAM.
+//
+// GS colour maths is done in the fragment shader in 0..255 units with the GS's integer truncation; blending uses
+// dual-source fixed-function blending where the GS equation allows, the rest reads the destination in the shader.
+#if defined(_WIN32)
+
+#ifndef WIN32_LEAN_AND_MEAN
+#define WIN32_LEAN_AND_MEAN
+#endif
